ude a subset of the full factorial combinations. This allows researchers to study the effects of several factors simultaneously while reducing the total number of experimental runs. The design layout typically involves selecting a fraction of the full factorial design based on specific criteria, such as resolution and the number of factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of AGJ and fractional factorial designs relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The effects of factors are additive.\n2. The errors are normally distributed and independent.\n3. The variance of errors is constant across all levels of the factors (homoscedasticity).\n4. The experimental runs are independent.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nAGJ is particularly applicable when:\n- The number of factors is large, making a full factorial design impractical.\n- The researcher seeks to identify significant factors and interactions with a limited number of runs.\n- Preliminary studies are needed to screen factors before conducting more detailed investigations.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data obtained from AGJ involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the factors and their interactions. The typical steps include:\n1. Model fitting using regression techniques.\n2. ANOVA to test the significance of main effects and interactions.\n3. Post-hoc analysis if necessary to explore significant factors further.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Reduces the number of experimental runs required, saving time and resources.\n- Allows for the identification of significant factors and interactions in complex experiments.\n- Provides a systematic approach to factor screening.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Potential for confounding effects, particularly in lower resolution designs.\n- Limited ability to estimate higher-order interactions accurately.\n- Requires careful selection of the fraction to ensure valid c